[^1]:  See [Snow.js](https://github.com/LavaMoat/snow) for a failed attempt at preventing access to non-overridden JS objects and [Realms-Initialization-Control](https://github.com/WICG/Realms-Initialization-Control) for a WICG web standards proposal that aims to make tamper-proof overrides possible in-browser.

[^2]:  Theoretically, ideal 1, “No spinners: your work at your fingertips,” can be broken by an application, but to do so an application would have to ask permission from the user on every single web request, which would significantly degrade the user experience of the application to a point where a user would likely choose to use a different application that offered similar functionality without pestering them with a popup on every keypress.

[^3]:  Note that a destination can either be a friend or a company and that either way the traffic should be fully encrypted in transit (either through https for sending data to a company’s api or end to end encryption for sending data to a friend).

[^4]:  This diagram was created entirely within the privacy sandbox using a local build of excalidraw.com

[^5]:  I have opened an issue petitioning for the `prefetch-src` CSP to be added back in order to have an in-browser mitigation for this information leak although it appears I personally would have to head the effort to add it back as it is a low priority for most browser implementers [15\], \[16\]. Such a task would take a significant amount of time and effort, effort that would not ultimately impact the net security of the in-browser sandbox without other additions to various other browser specifications to address the other 3 exfiltration vectors listed throughout this thesis.

[^6]:  The current implementation only features actual functionality for localStorage. Accessing any of the other storage mechanisms from within the iframe will fail as though the browser itself does not support the storage feature. While ideally all of the APIs would be implemented in the prototype, only localStorage is really necessary for a proof of concept implementation as ultimately the other storage mechanisms merely provide nicer ergonomics compared to localStorage for persistent data storage.

[^7]:  see [Snow.js](https://github.com/LavaMoat/snow) for a failed attempt at preventing access to non-overridden JS objects and [Realms-Initialization-Control](https://github.com/WICG/Realms-Initialization-Control) for a WICG web standards proposal that aims to address this issue.

[^8]:  “Progressive Web Apps,” which are installed through the browser and which ultimately are simple websites with slightly expanded capabilities, do not have to worry about data eviction as the are supposed to be treated the same as native applications in terms of capabilities.
